WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.723
Python applications often connect user-controlled data to powerful interpreters: database engines, operating system shells, template engines, expression evaluators, and plugin systems.

2
00:00:12.723 --> 00:00:15.671
The mistake is not using these capabilities.

3
00:00:15.671 --> 00:00:22.808
The mistake is allowing untrusted or poorly constrained data to influence instructions rather than values.

4
00:00:22.808 --> 00:00:31.108
When code builds commands or queries as strings, it becomes harder to prove that data remains data and control remains control.

5
00:00:31.108 --> 00:00:38.866
For databases, safer patterns include parameterized queries, prepared statements, and careful use of ORM features.

6
00:00:38.866 --> 00:00:43.986
An ORM can reduce risk, but it does not make every query pattern safe by default.

7
00:00:43.986 --> 00:00:51.589
Raw query escape hatches, dynamic filters, string-built clauses, and flexible search features still need review.

8
00:00:51.589 --> 00:00:59.347
The stable principle is to use APIs that separate query structure from data, then review any exception with extra care.

9
00:00:59.347 --> 00:01:02.372
Command execution needs the same discipline.

10
00:01:02.372 --> 00:01:09.432
Many tasks are safer when handled by Python libraries instead of shelling out to operating system commands.

11
00:01:09.432 --> 00:01:21.301
When subprocess behavior is required, developers should understand the difference between passing controlled argument lists and invoking a shell that interprets a command line.

12
00:01:21.301 --> 00:01:25.800
Untrusted values should not be allowed to become command structure.

13
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:34.722
Allowlists, constrained options, safe working directories, clear timeouts, and careful error handling are part of defensive design.

14
00:01:34.722 --> 00:01:44.962
Dynamic execution features such as eval, exec, dynamic imports, plugin loading, and server-side template evaluation should be rare and heavily reviewed.

15
00:01:44.962 --> 00:01:52.797
They can be useful in constrained systems, but they increase the cost of reasoning about security and maintainability.

16
00:01:52.797 --> 00:02:02.262
If a feature needs dynamic behavior, prefer narrow interfaces, explicit allowlists, data-driven configuration, and safe evaluation models.

17
00:02:02.262 --> 00:02:07.847
Clever code is not valuable if the team cannot secure it, test it, and explain it later.
